  "panel_source": "M-II (Rhythm & Groove Motor)",
  "panel_reasoning_excerpt": "### Template M12: BRECVEMA Multi-Mechanism Framework\n\n**Core claim**: Music evokes emotion through at least eight distinct psychological mechanisms that operate in parallel, each with different evolutionary origins, neural substrates, developmental trajectories, speed of activation, and dependence on musical knowledge. The felt emotion at any moment is the resultant of all currently active mechanisms.\n\n**Mechanisms and substrates**:\n- B (Brainstem reflex): Brainstem nuclei \u2192 autonomic response. Speed: ~50ms. Knowledge required: None.\n- R (Rhythmic entrainment): BG + motor cortex \u2192 autonomic and motor coupling. Speed: ~100-500ms. Knowledge: None.\n- E (Evaluative conditioning): Amygdala + hippocampus \u2192 conditioned association. Speed: ~500ms. Knowledge: None (experience-dependent).\n- C (Emotional contagion): Mirror system + insula \u2192 empathic mirroring. Speed: ~200-500ms. Knowledge: Minimal.\n- V (Visual imagery): Visual cortex + PFC \u2192 imagery-mediated emotion. Speed: ~1-5s. Knowledge: Minimal.\n- E (Episodic memory): Hippocampus + DMN \u2192 memory-triggered emotion. Speed: ~1-5s. Knowledge: None (experience-dependent).\n- M (Musical expectancy): Auditory cortex \u2192 IFG \u2192 striatum \u2192 PE-mediated emotion. Speed: ~200ms-2s. Knowledge: Yes (implicit statistical learning).\n- A (Aesthetic judgment): PFC + vmPFC/OFC \u2192 evaluative emotion. Speed: ~2-10s. Knowledge: Yes (implicit or explicit).\n\n**Formal structure**:\n- Emotion(t) = \u03a3_i w_i(t) \u00d7 mechanism_i(t), where i \u2208 {B, R, E_c, C, V, E_m, M, A}\n- w_i(t) = weighting of mechanism i at time t (depends on listener, context, and musical features)\n- Mechanisms activate at different latencies and may produce same-signed or opposite-signed affective signals\n- Compound emotions arise when mechanisms produce conflicting valence signals simultaneously\n\n**Key insight**: Musical emotion is irreducibly multi-mechanism. Single-mechanism theories (emotion = prediction error; emotion = acoustic features; emotion = memory) each capture one pathway and miss the rest. The BRECVEMA framework integrates them.\n\n**Maturity**: \u2605\u2605\u2605 (Comprehensive taxonomy with strong face validity; empirical isolation of individual mechanisms still limited; formal model of mechanism interaction not yet specified)\n\n**Key references**: Juslin (2013); Juslin & V\u00e4stfj\u00e4ll (2008); Juslin et al. (2010)",
